# ---- Required ----

# Encrypts everyone's settings into their link. 32+ characters; make one with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Keep it (and back it up): changing or losing it breaks every link people have made.
CONFIG_SECRET=

# This addon's address as AIOStreams and players reach it, no trailing slash. Links and
# subtitle download URLs are built on it. Example: https://subs.example.com
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# ---- Your AIOStreams (recommended) ----

# Your own AIOStreams' public address: install URLs from it are accepted for sync.
# OWN_AIOSTREAMS=https://aiostreams.example.com
# Where this container reaches it, if not at that address (for example on the same Docker network).
# Defaults to OWN_AIOSTREAMS.
# AIOSTREAMS_BASE=http://aiostreams:3000
# The other AIOStreams instances whose install URLs are accepted, comma-separated. Defaults to the
# public instances from the community Jellyfin guide. Set it to your own to accept only that one.
# ALLOWED_AIOSTREAMS=https://aiostreams.example.com

# ---- Who can open the pages ----
# Each opens a sign-in page that keeps you signed in for 30 days. The addon paths never ask for one.

# Required, 8+ characters. Signs in to /dashboard: usage, API limits, what was played, configurations and settings.
# Keep it to yourself.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# Optional. Asked for on /configure, where people make and edit their links; give it to the friends
# you add. Unset means anyone who can reach /configure can make a link (fine behind a proxy login).
# ADDON_PASSWORD=

# ---- Optional ----
# ADDON_PASSWORD, the AIOStreams settings above and every setting below but PORT, CACHE_DIR and
# CAPTIONS_DIR can also be changed on the dashboard (Settings), without a restart. What's set here
# stays the default they reset to.

# The clock the dashboard shows times on (an IANA name such as Europe/London). Default UTC.
# TIME_ZONE=UTC
# Most GB read from the files being played in a day (UTC), over everyone; syncing new files waits
# for the next day once it's spent. Default 20; 0 for no limit.
# DAILY_READ_GB=20
# Hosts on a private network that playing files may be read from, comma-separated, with or without a
# port: a stream proxy on your LAN, say. Without it a file is read only from the public internet and
# from your own AIOStreams (OWN_AIOSTREAMS, AIOSTREAMS_BASE).
# PRIVATE_STREAM_HOSTS=proxy.lan,192.168.1.9:8080
# How long a subtitle list may take before it answers with what it has, in ms (default 8000). It can
# take 1.5 s more on a first play, so this addon's timeout in AIOStreams (7000 unless changed) must be
# at least this plus 2500, or AIOStreams drops the whole list. Add to AIOStreams on the configure page
# sets that timeout; the page names it for adding the addon by hand.
# LIST_BUDGET_MS=8000
# FILE_WAIT_MS=1500
# AniDB (optional): with a client registered at anidb.net (Add Project, then an HTTP API client), an
# anime that gets no season map from the Anime Kitsu addon gets one from AniDB's titles, so a subtitle
# named for another season isn't taken for the one playing. The name is lower case, the version a
# whole number. AniDB is asked slowly and rarely: one request every 4 s at most, 100 a day at most,
# an anime once in 90 days, and nothing for 36 hours after any refusal.
# ANIDB_CLIENT=yourclientname
# ANIDB_CLIENT_VER=1
# OpenSubtitles' own API key, one for the whole server (free with an account at opensubtitles.com).
# With it, OpenSubtitles is listed from its own search, which marks machine and AI translations;
# without it, OpenSubtitles V3+ lists, which carries no such mark. Only searches use the key;
# subtitles are still downloaded through OpenSubtitles V3+.
# OPENSUBTITLES_API_KEY=
# Anime titles without an account: when the Anime Kitsu addon gives an anime no season map and no
# AniDB client is set above, its seasons' titles are read from the anime-offline-database (a public
# dataset on GitHub, ODbL 1.0), downloaded the first time a show needs it (about 5 MB) and looked up
# again once a day at most. Default on; off never downloads it.
# ANIME_TITLES_DATASET=on
# The most subtitles a configuration may list for one language, 1 to 3 (default 3). A configuration
# lists one unless it asks for more; each one listed is downloaded and timed on every play.
# MAX_SUBTITLES_PER_LANGUAGE=3
# How long subtitle search results are kept, in seconds (default 6 hours).
# RESULT_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=21600
# Once a day, ask GitHub whether a newer release is out, so the dashboard can say so. It sends nothing
# about this server. Default on; off never asks.
# UPDATE_CHECK=on
# Days of log files kept in the cache volume (logs/), today included, so the log outlives an update; 0 keeps
# none. LOG_MAX_MB caps their total size (the oldest day goes first).
# LOG_DAYS=7
# LOG_MAX_MB=50
# PORT=7000

# Where the cache is kept, inside the container (the image sets "cache"; ".cache" outside it).
# CACHE_DIR=cache

# Subtitles of your own (README, "Subtitles kept on your server"): the folder they are in, inside the
# container. Mount a folder there in docker-compose.yml to use it.
# CAPTIONS_DIR=captions
